course: dictionary
lesson: 2515
title: Decidedly: clearly and firmly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Decidedly is an adverb. Adverbs tell you more about how something is or how someone does something. It has two main meanings: clearly and without any doubt, or very much. «The passage is long, gloomy, and, to tell the truth, decidedly shabby» means the passage is clearly and certainly shabby.
explain: The second meaning is about a firm way of speaking or acting, when someone has made up their mind. «"No," she said decidedly» means she said no in a firm way, with no wobble in her voice. Swap in the word clearly or firmly. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Look at the words around decidedly for clues. In «other tunnels branch off from a central hall, mysterious and without apparent end», the words around it show that the mood is strong and certain. Context helps you decide which meaning fits best.
fact: Decidedly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is or how someone acts.
fact: «Decidedly» means clearly and without any doubt, or very much: «it was decidedly colder».
fact: «Decidedly» can also mean in a firm way, when someone has made up their mind: «"No," she said decidedly».
fact: To check the meaning, swap in clearly or firmly and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Stories such as Alice in Wonderland use decidedly to show that a character is sure of herself.
exercise: «It was decidedly colder this morning.» What does decidedly mean here? | *Clearly and without any doubt, very much | Quietly and slowly | Only just | Happily, with a smile
exercise: «"No," she said decidedly.» What does decidedly show about Alice? | *She had made up her mind and was firm | She was lost in the tunnels | She was joking and laughing | She was asleep and dreaming
exercise: «The passage is long, gloomy, and, to tell the truth, decidedly shabby.» Find and copy the word that shows the shabbiness is clear and certain. | long | passage | gloomy | *decidedly
test: «Alice spoke decidedly when she was sure of herself.» What does decidedly mean in this sentence? | *In a firm way, showing she was sure | Without any reason at all | Hiding from others | Very slowly and quietly
test: Ben says decidedly means only a bit. Why is he wrong? | It is a name for a town | It means in a secret way | *It means clearly, or very much, so it is much stronger than a bit | It means a little bit, so he is right
exam: «The tunnel was decidedly dark, and the air was cold.» How do you know decidedly means clearly and without doubt? | *It is a verb that means to run away | It is a strong word that shows the darkness is certain, and swapping in clearly still makes sense | It means the air was warm and sunny | It means the tunnel had a door, so it must be a house
